# The SAFETY direction of the known-length fact: an access that is NOT
# proven in bounds must still be checked.
#
# The compiler knows a literal array's exact length, so a literal index can
# be proven in range (and the check dropped). This file is the other side:
# the proof must NOT fire when it should not, and the check must still
# trap. The compiler cannot be relied on to notice a mistake here -- the
# test asserts the program ABORTS.
#
# (The trap is the runtime's bounds check, so this is a runtime assertion,
# not a compile-time one. The run.sh check is therefore on the OUTPUT of a
# run that is expected to fail, which is why this fixture lives in
# tests_manual/ and is exercised by tests/test_bounds_safety.sh.)

fn main() -> i64 {
    a = [10, 20, 30]
    print(a[3])          # out of range: must trap, not read past the end
    return 0
}
